# GC21 Upper Respiratory Tract Infections <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

- **Learning objectives:**
  - Define URTIs
  - Epidemiology
  - Problem solving of common respiratory symptoms
  - Diagnosis of URTIs
  - Evidence-based management of URTIs
- **Definition of URTIs:**
  - <u>Clinical definition</u> - self-limited irritation and/or swelling of the upper airway a/w cough and no signs of pneumonia, in a patient with no other conditions accounting for their symptoms, or with no history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease
  - <u>Pathological definition</u> - infection of the mucous membranes of the upper respiratory tract from the nostrils to the larynx, and includes the paranasal sinuses and the middle ear
- **Classification of URTIs:**
  - <u>By anatomical classification/ Clinical syndrome</u>:
    - **Nasal vestibulitis** - viral/ bacterial
    - **Acute rhinosinusitis** - S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis
    - **Tonsilitis** - viral/ bacterial
    - **Acute coryza/ Common cold** (non-specific infection of upper tract) - rhinovirus, adenovirus, RSV
    - **Flu** - influenza
    - **Pharyngitis** - GAS, viral cause
    - **Laryngitis** - viral/ bacterial
    - **Otitis media** - S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, M. cattarhalis
  - <u>By etiology</u>:
    - **Viral** (\> 90%) - rhinovirus, adenovirus, influenza virus, parainfluenza virus, respiratory syncytial virus, coronavirus and SARS-COV2
    - **Bacterial** (\< 10%) - S. pyogenes, H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, M. catarrhalis, S. aureus etc.
- **Epidemiology:**
  - <u>Prevalence</u> - the most common infection in human
  - <u>Incidence</u>:
    - Adults - 2-3x/y
    - Children - up to 8x/y
  - <u>Burden</u> - self-limiting disease but with significant health and economic burden:
    - **Economic burden** - panpower loss, missed school days
    - **Healthcare burden** - ~900000/y attendance in public primary care clinics (?source)
  - <u>Reason for consultation</u> (significant cause of patient visits) - non-lethal and short in duration, typically present in hopes of **symptomatic relief** (some consult due to worry about non-infective etiology)
- **Transmission of acute coryzal illness** (Lorber B. JGIM 1996):
  - <u>Route of transmission</u> - contact (virus found in 40% hand samples) and droplet transmission
  - <u>Natural infectivity</u> - variable (38-88%)
  - <u>Natural Hx</u> - 3d median incubation period (1-10d), maximal viral shedding at D2-3 of illness
- **Clinical presentation** - 1-3d after exposure, lasts 7-10d, can persist up to 3w:
  - **Cough** (2-20d) - acute irritating cough with scanty sputum, both in waking and nocturnal, with absence of S/S of pneumonia or other conditions explaining the Sx
    - <u>Onset</u> - acute in onset, but self-limiting
    - <u>Progression</u> - self-limiting, lasting 1-3 weeks
    - <u>Quality</u> - irritating, scanty whitish, yellowish sputum
    - <u>Timing</u> - daytime, just before falling asleep and upon waking, but a bit calmer when sleeping
  - **Rhinorrhoea and nasal congestion** (2-14d) - yellow nasal discharge, but color does not inform whether viral or bacterial (N.B. not all rhinorhea are URTI, esp. if unilateral):
    - <u>Onset</u> - acute onset
    - <u>Progression</u> - self-limiting, lasting 3-5 days
    - <u>Quality</u> - copious clear watery/ yellow discharge, with sneezing++, and relatively little itchiness (cf allergic rhinitis)
    - <u>Timing</u> - little diurnal variation
  - **Sore throat** (2-14d) - most (\> 90%) are due to viral URTIs, but must consider acute epiglottitis, bacterial tonsillitis, deep neck space infections, TB, lymphoma
  - **Malaise and tiredness** - may be marked in some indivudals
  - **Low-grade fever** - high-grade fever suggestive of other causes
  - **Other possible symptoms:**
    - Headache (1-14d) - prominent Sx in influenza
    - Myalgia (1-14d) - prominent Sx in influenza
    - Facial pressure - reflects rhinosinusitis
    - Hoarseness (2-20d) - indicative of laryngeal involvement
  - **Absence of lower respiratory tract S/S** - e.g. no SOB, absent chest signs (e.g. consolidations, crackles etc.)
- **P/E:**
  - <u>General examination</u> - initial clinical assessment to look for potential medical emergencies (e.g. toxic/ septic appearance, drooling, cyanosis, SOB, wheezing)
  - <u>Vital signs</u> - BP/P (change in HR concordant to fever), SpO2
  - <u>Respiratory examination</u> (auscultate the chest) - wheeze (asthma atack), focal crackles (pneumonia)
- **Principles of Ix** - dependent on clinical suspicion and whether it alters Mx (e.g. influenza RAT, throat swab based on CENTOR score)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx** - adequate rest and hydration most effective:
    - <u>Patient education</u> - role of rest, hydration and sleep
    - <u>Symptomatic Tx</u> - evidence-based Tx for symptomatic relief, but tailored to patient's prference (not a pil for every ill)
    - <u>Infection control</u> - good personal hygiene to avoid transmission to others (e.g. hand washing, mask-wearing)
    - <u>Curative Tx</u> - antivirals reserved for selected patients
  - **Patient education:**
    - <u>Role of adequate rest and sleep</u> - inadequate or inefficient sleep is independently associated w/ development of a cold
  - **Symptomatic relief for systemic Sx and sore throat:**
    - <u>Selection</u> - paracetamol and NSAID are first-line Tx:
      - Paracetamol - most evidence to be effective and safe
      - NSAID (e.g. ibuprofen) - new evidence demonstrated to be more effected, but S/E profiles are worrisome (large trials demonstrated similar safety profiles in children likely due to short course)
      - Aspirin - C/I in children to avoid Reye's syndrome
      - Throat lozenges - no quality trials demonstrating effectiveness
      - Steroids - no role in simple URTI (harm \> benefits)
  - **Symptomatic relief for cough** - no demonstrable effectiveness in OTC cough mixtures, reassurance of expected course of cough up to 3 weeks:
    - <u>Indications and C/I</u> - generally not indicated if cough not severely distressing due to S/E profiles:
      - FDA - no cough and cold product that contains decongestant or antihistamines for \<2y
      - Health Canada/NHS - no OTC cough and cold medicine for \< 6y
    - <u>Selection</u>:
      - **Mucolytics** (Bromhexine, acetylcysteine) - reduced surface tension and viscosity of mucus
      - **Sedating Antihistamines** (dexbrompheniramine, diphenhydramine) - no proven benefit but S/E profile related to increased parasympathetic stimulation (e.g. dry mouth, blurred vision, AROU)
      - **Cough expectorants** (MES. Cocillana compound syrup) - no proven effectiveness by RCT, and can result in N/V
      - **Opioid** (codeine, pholcodine, dextromethorphan) - absolutely not indicated in URTI
      - **Inhaled beta agonists** - may be effective for prolonged cough esp. in patients w/ airway hypersensitivity, but rarely used
  - **Symptomatic relief for nasal symptoms:**
    - <u>Evidence</u> - no proven efficacy in children and adult use (avoid in children \< 2y)
    - <u>Selection</u>:
      - **Antihistamines** - non-sedative antihistamines are much less effective, while chlorpheniramine is much more effective but can cause marked sedation
      - **Nsal decongestants** (e.g. pseudoephedrine, phenylpropanolamine) - transient relief of congestive Sx but S/E common esp. hypertensive
      - **Topical ipratropium** - effective but expensive
      - **Heated humidified air** - benign and possibly beneficial
  - **Disease modifying Tx:**
    - <u>Vitamin C 1-3g</u> - no effect if taken at onset of disease
    - <u>Zinc (\>75mg/d)</u> - may shorten illness by 1-3d, but most effective if started within 24h of onset of Sx
  - **Curative Tx** - antivirals used in certain populations (see notes in Influenza and COVID)
- **Approach to cough and sputum:**
  - <u>Initial clinical assessment</u>:
    - Signs of emergency - cyanosis, dyspnoea (ability to talk in full sentences), drooling
  - HPI - OPQRST:
    - O - acute vs subacute vs recurrent, chronic
    - Q - red is the only important colour in sputum
- Approach to sore throat:
  - Etiology - viral URTI, acute epiglotitis, tonsillitis, deep neck space infections, lymphoma, postnasal drip, FB, IM (see slides)
  - CENTOR Criteria for sore throat:
    - Clinical use - estimates probability that the pharyngitis is S. pyogenes
    - 4 criteria - 1 score for each +ve criterion:
      - History of fever
      - Tonsilar exudates
      - Tender anterior cervical adenopathy
      - Absence of cough
    - Modified CENTOR Criteria - adds age to criteria:
      - 3-14y - add 1 point
      - \>45 y - subtract 1 point
    - Scores - ranges from 1-5
    - Approach to Mx:
      - -1 to 1 point - no culture and no ABx
      - 2-5 - consider rapid strep testing +/- culture and ABx if +ve (old guidelines say treat empirically if \> 4)
- Hoarseness - indicates pathology in the larynx:
  - Etiology:
    - Viral URTI - acute laryngitis esp. if acute
    - Vocal cord polyps/ nodules
    - Acute epiglottitis
    - Croup
    - Laryngeal neoplasms
    - Laryngeal injury - e.g. external compression, trauma from intubation
